CH = dict(num=15, tag="Political Science · Ch 3", theme="Social divisions and how they shape politics: gender, religion and caste",
 title="Gender, Religion and Caste",
 intro="Social differences of gender, religion and caste affect politics in every democracy. Politics can help to reduce inequalities or can be used to deepen divisions. This chapter shows how each division operates in India, what the Constitution says and what citizens should guard against.",
 big_questions=["How does gender division affect politics?", "What is communalism and a secular state?", "How do caste and politics interact?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Gender and Politics"),
 ("p", "Gender division is a form of hierarchical social division based on gender, but it is generally not recognised in politics as a division. It is mostly based not on biology but on social expectations and stereotypes."),
 ("ul", ["**Public/private division:** Boys and girls are brought up to believe that the main responsibility of women is housework and bringing up children. This is reflected in a **sexual division of labour** in most families: women do all work inside the home, such as cooking, cleaning, washing, tailoring and looking after children, while men do the work outside. Women also work outside the home (fetching water, collecting fuel, working in fields, as domestic helpers or in offices), but this work is not valued and does not get recognition.", "Women do not have equal chances: **literacy rate** among women is only about 54 per cent against 76 per cent for men (figures given in the NCERT text); girls drop out because parents spend more on boys' education; an Indian woman works about one hour more than a man every day, yet much of this work is unpaid; women are paid less than men even for the same work, though the **Equal Remuneration Act, 1976** requires equal wages for equal work; the **child sex ratio** fell to just **919 girls per 1000 boys** (Census 2011) because of sex-selective abortion.", "Many women's movements have demanded equal rights in personal and family life, in education and in the workplace; feminist movements have raised the issue of women's political representation. In countries like **Sweden, Norway and Finland**, the participation of women in public life is high.", "**Political representation:** In India, women are far from adequately represented in legislatures: the share of elected women in the Lok Sabha touched **14.36 per cent** of its strength for the first time in 2019, while their share in state assemblies is **less than 5 per cent**; India is in the bottom group of nations in this respect. One way to ensure this is to have **reservation**: **one-third of seats** in panchayats and municipalities are reserved for women (more than 10 lakh elected women representatives). The **Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam (2023)** provides for **33 per cent reservation of seats for women** in the Lok Sabha, State Legislative Assemblies and the Delhi Assembly."]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "Feminist movements and laws such as the **Equal Remuneration Act, 1976** have attempted to secure equal pay; women in India work for long hours but their contribution is under-counted."),
 ("h2", "Religion, Communalism and Politics"),
 ("p", "Religious differences are not the same as communalism. Religion can be part of politics, e.g. when it is concerned with values and ethics: **Gandhiji** said that religion can never be separated from politics, by which he meant moral values drawn from all religions, not any particular community. Human rights groups have demanded that the government take special steps to prevent communal riots and protect religious minorities; women's movements argue that family laws of all religions discriminate against women."),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Communalism** is the belief that religion is the principal basis of social community; the followers of a particular religion must belong to one community, their fundamental interests are the same, and any difference they have is irrelevant or trivial to that community. It leads to the idea that people of different religions cannot belong to the same social community."),
 ("ul", ["**Forms of communalism:** in everyday beliefs (religious prejudices, stereotypes, belief in the superiority of one's religion); in a political form, by using sacred symbols and religious leaders and seeking votes on religious grounds; by **communal violence** and riots, as during the **Partition** in 1947 and in later years.", "**Secular state:** The makers of India's Constitution chose the **secular** path. There is **no official religion**; the state does not give a special status to any religion; people are free to follow, practise and propagate any religion or not follow any; the Constitution prohibits **discrimination** on grounds of religion; yet the state can intervene in religious matters to ensure equality, e.g. by banning untouchability."]),
 ("h2", "Caste and Politics"),
 ("ul", ["**Caste inequalities:** In most societies, some form of social division and social inequalities exist; in India caste has been unique through its **hereditary occupational division** sanctioned by rituals. Members of the same caste group are supposed to form a social community that practises the same or similar occupation, marries within the caste and excludes members of other caste groups from eating with them.", "**Reformers and thinkers:** **Jyotirao Phule, Mahatma Gandhi, B. R. Ambedkar and Periyar E. V. Ramaswami Naicker** advocated and worked to establish a society in which caste inequalities are absent.", "**Changes:** Partly due to their efforts and partly due to other social and economic changes such as **urbanisation, growth of literacy and education, occupational mobility** and weakening of the position of landlords, the old notions of caste hierarchy are breaking down. The Constitution prohibited any caste-based discrimination and laid the foundations of policies to reverse the injustices; yet caste is still in the way people marry, and untouchability has not ended completely."]),
 ("h3", "Caste in politics"),
 ("ul", ["When parties choose candidates for elections, they take into account the caste composition of the electorate; governments are formed with a mix of castes; parties and candidates make appeals to caste sentiment, and universal adult franchise and the principle of ‘one person one vote’ have forced political leaders to gain support of various groups.", "Politics works to bring **under-privileged communities** into the political arena, and many castes, including Dalits, have gained greater voice and power. Caste has also opened the way for people to claim their share.", "**Limits:** No parliamentary constituency has a clear majority of one caste, so parties cannot win by winning one caste; voters have other interests too, such as the performance of the government and the party they prefer, and many candidates win by gaining support across castes.", "**Politicisation of caste** can lead to tension, violence and divisiveness, and diverts attention from vital issues like poverty, development and corruption; hence caste-based politics is not good for a democracy."]),
 ("table", ["Division", "Positive side", "Danger"], [["Gender", "Women's movements, reservation in local bodies, Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam", "Patriarchy, low representation, wage gap"], ["Religion", "Ethics and value-based politics, secular Constitution", "Communalism, riots, majority-minority conflict"], ["Caste", "Voice for Dalits and OBCs, social reform, reservation", "Casteism, violence, neglect of development issues"]]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What is sexual division of labour?", "Work in the home done by women, and work outside done by men."),
  ("Why is women's representation in legislatures low?", "Social attitudes, family duties and a political system that is male-dominated."),
  ("What is communalism?", "Belief that religion is the main basis of community and politics."),
  ("What does a secular state mean?", "No official religion, equality of all faiths, and no discrimination."),
  ("Who worked to end caste inequalities?", "Phule, Gandhi, Ambedkar and Periyar."),
  ("Why are caste-based politics limited?", "No constituency has a single-caste majority; voters also look at party and candidate."),
  ("What does the Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam provide?", "One-third reservation for women in Lok Sabha and state assemblies."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Gender division is shaped by social roles, not biology; women are under-represented in politics.", "**Communalism** uses religion for political ends; India has a **secular** Constitution.", "Caste is a social hierarchy that has been changed by reform, education and the Constitution.", "Caste and politics interact: they help voice of the marginalised but can divide society.", "A healthy democracy uses social differences for inclusion and not conflict."])
